body_text:
### Describe the bug

A query fails when a table follows an `UNNEST` and is linked to the `UNNEST` output (or to the model right
before the next outer join), with the `UNNEST` in the same FROM list as its source table. The query is
valid, but QuestDB fails with `AssertionError: UNNEST requires a master factory` under `-ea`. Without
`-ea`, it fails with a `NullPointerException` in `SqlCodeGenerator.generateUnnest`
(`masterFactory.getMetadata()`). EXPLAIN fails the same way.

`SqlOptimiser` gives an `UNNEST` model no ordering edge to the tables its expressions read. The model
stays out of the ordering graph and `doReorderTables` appends it after its source table, in model
order, only as long as nothing links it. Any edge out of it makes it an ordering root: a join key that
reads an `UNNEST` column (`JOIN d ON d.x = u.v`, or `WHERE g.b = u.v`), or the dependency that
`addOuterJoinExpression` adds from the model before an outer join to that join
(`linkDependencies(parent, joinIndex - 1, joinIndex)`). `doReorderTables` then runs the `UNNEST` first,
before its source table (`f0` below) has joined, and `SqlCodeGenerator.generateJoins` reaches the
`JOIN_UNNEST` branch with `master == null`.

Seen on master 641aa5fee5. Wrapping the `UNNEST` in a sub-query works around it.

### To reproduce

```sql
CREATE TABLE f0 (a0 INT, arr0 DOUBLE[], ts TIMESTAMP) TIMESTAMP(ts);
INSERT INTO f0 VALUES (1, ARRAY[1.0, 2.0], '2024-01-01T00:00:01'), (2, ARRAY[3.0], '2024-01-01T00:00:03');
CREATE TABLE f1 (a1 INT, arr1 DOUBLE[], ts TIMESTAMP) TIMESTAMP(ts);
INSERT INTO f1 VALUES (1, ARRAY[0.5], '2024-01-01T00:00:00'), (3, ARRAY[2.5, 4.0], '2024-01-01T00:00:02');
CREATE TABLE g1 (b1 DOUBLE, k1 INT);
INSERT INTO g1 VALUES (1.0, 1), (2.5, 3);
CREATE TABLE d (k INT, x DOUBLE);
INSERT INTO d VALUES (1, 1.5), (3, 2.5), (2, 3.5);

-- all fail with "UNNEST requires a master factory"
SELECT * FROM f0, UNNEST(f0.arr0) u(v) JOIN d ON d.x = u.v;
SELECT * FROM f0, UNNEST(f0.arr0) u(v) LEFT JOIN d ON d.x = u.v;
SELECT * FROM f0, UNNEST(f0.arr0) u(v) LEFT JOIN d ON d.x > u.v;
SELECT * FROM f0, UNNEST(f0.arr0) u(v) CROSS JOIN g1 WHERE g1.b1 = u.v;
SELECT * FROM f0 CROSS JOIN f1, UNNEST(f1.arr1) u(v) LEFT JOIN d ON d.x > u.v;
SELECT * FROM f0 CROSS JOIN g1, UNNEST(f0.arr0) u(v) LEFT JOIN d ON d.x > g1.b1;
```

The sub-query form of the first query returns the expected empty result (no `v` equals a `d.x`), and
`SELECT * FROM (SELECT * FROM f0, UNNEST(f0.arr0) u(v)) t LEFT JOIN d ON d.x > t.v` returns 6 rows.

### Expected fix direction

Give every `UNNEST` model an ordering edge from each model its `UNNEST` expressions read, the way
`createImpliedDependencies` gives a time-series join a context parent 0. The `UNNEST` then stays after
its source whatever edges leave it. This changes the join order, and so the plans, of `UNNEST`
queries that work today (for example `t, UNNEST(t.arr) u JOIN t2 ON t2.id = t.id` would run the
`UNNEST` before the hash join), so plan assertions in `UnnestTest` need a review.

### QuestDB version

master (641aa5fee5); also branch fix/join-optimiser-lost-rows (db69fb3f4a)

Found while working on #7674.

