body_text:
### Describe the bug

A LATERAL sub-query is equivalent to running its body once per outer row, with the outer columns substituted. QuestDB rewrites it into joins instead (`LateralJoinRewriter`). The 16 shapes below do not survive that rewrite. Each is a separate defect; they are collected here because they sit in the same code and were found together. None of them needs a RIGHT or FULL join in the body (those are tracked in #7723, #7724 and #7726).

- Wrong rows without an error: sections 1 to 8, 11 and 12.
- Internal columns (`__lateral_rn`, `_latest_rn`, `column`) in the result set: section 9.
- Valid queries that fail: sections 3, 10 and 13 to 16.

**Impact:** silent wrong results (missing, extra and misattributed rows, wrong values and column types); internal columns in result sets and in tables created with `CREATE TABLE AS`; valid queries that fail with `NullPointerException`, `AssertionError`, `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`, `UnsupportedOperationException`, or with an error that names a generated column at position 0. Two of the failure paths (sections 3 and 13) leave native memory allocated on every failed query.

Reproduced on master 4b67b0cdc4 (`LateralJoinRewriter.java` has not changed on master since; checked at 6c63556431) and on the PR #7618 branch (0dd219583d), with and without `-ea`. Both give the same result unless a section says otherwise. Stack traces are from master 4b67b0cdc4.

The expected rows come from running the sub-query once per outer row with the outer values substituted as literals, on QuestDB itself. Every section is self-contained: it creates its own tables.

### 1. The ON clause of the LATERAL join is ignored when the sub-query's correlation is not equalities only

```sql
CREATE TABLE o (id INT, k INT, x INT, y INT);
INSERT INTO o VALUES (1, 1, 1, 0), (2, 2, 1, 0);
CREATE TABLE p (k INT, x INT, y INT, v INT);
INSERT INTO p VALUES (1, 1, 5, 10), (2, 1, 5, 20);

-- Q1
SELECT o.id, t.v FROM o JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT v, k jk FROM p WHERE x = o.x AND y > o.y
) t ON t.jk = o.k
ORDER BY 1, 2;

-- Q2: a constant in the ON clause
SELECT o.id, t.v FROM o JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT v, k jk FROM p WHERE x = o.x AND y > o.y
) t ON t.jk = 1
ORDER BY 1, 2;
```

| Query | Expected | Actual |
|---|---|---|
| Q1 | `1 10`, `2 20` | `1 10`, `1 20`, `2 10`, `2 20` |
| Q2 | `1 10`, `2 10` | `1 10`, `1 20`, `2 10`, `2 20` |

The plan of Q1 has no trace of `t.jk = o.k`:

```
Encode sort
  keys: [id, v]
    SelectedRecord
        Filter filter: o.y<t.y
            Hash Join Light
              condition: t.x=o.x
                PageFrame
                    Row forward scan
                    Frame forward scan on: o
                Hash
                    PageFrame
                        Row forward scan
                        Frame forward scan on: p
```

- `LEFT JOIN LATERAL (...) t ON t.jk = o.k` returns the same four rows.
- With `WHERE x = o.x` alone (no `AND y > o.y`), Q1 returns the expected rows.

Likely location: `LateralJoinRewriter.tryEliminateOuterRefInBranch()`. When not every outer column has an equality partner, it rebuilds the join criteria from the correlated predicates and replaces the whole ON clause of the join model (`joinModel.setJoinCriteria(simpleChainCriteria)`). #7725 describes this replacement for a join inside the body and assumes that the ON clause of the LATERAL join itself holds only the key alignment. A user-written ON clause on the LATERAL join is lost the same way.

### 2. A correlated aggregate without GROUP BY reads as NULL, or loses the outer row, when a filter or a join reads it

For an outer row without matching rows, `count(*)` is 0 and the sub-query still has one row. The result is right only when the aggregate goes straight to the projection.

```sql
CREATE TABLE o (id INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO o VALUES (1, 1), (2, 9);
CREATE TABLE p (k INT, v INT);
INSERT INTO p VALUES (0, 10);
CREATE TABLE c (x INT);
INSERT INTO c VALUES (1);
CREATE TABLE s (k INT, v INT);
INSERT INTO s VALUES (0, 500), (1, 501);

-- Q1: a filter above the aggregate
SELECT o.id, t.cnt FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT cnt FROM (SELECT count(*)::INT cnt FROM c WHERE x = o.x) WHERE cnt < 5
) t ORDER BY 1;

-- Q2: a join that reads the aggregate
SELECT o.id, t.cnt, t.sv FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT n1.cnt, n2.v sv
    FROM (SELECT count(*)::INT cnt FROM c WHERE x = o.x) n1 JOIN s n2 ON n2.k = n1.cnt
) t ORDER BY 1;

-- Q3: the aggregate as a joined sub-query, read by a later join
SELECT o.id, t.pv, t.cnt, t.sv FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT p.v pv, r.cnt, s.v sv FROM p
    LEFT JOIN (SELECT count(*)::INT cnt FROM c WHERE x = o.x) r ON true
    JOIN s ON s.k = r.cnt
) t ORDER BY 1;

-- Q4: the aggregate read by its own ON clause
SELECT o.id, t.pv, t.cnt FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT p.v pv, r.cnt FROM p
    LEFT JOIN (SELECT count(*)::INT cnt FROM c WHERE x = o.x) r ON r.cnt = p.k
) t ORDER BY 1;
```

| Query | Expected | Actual |
|---|---|---|
| Q1 | `1 1`, `2 0` | `1 1` |
| Q2 | `1 1 501`, `2 0 500` | `1 1 501` |
| Q3 | `1 10 1 501`, `2 10 0 500` | `1 10 1 501` |
| Q4 | `1 10 null`, `2 10 0` | `1 10 null`, `2 10 null` |

- Q3 with `LEFT JOIN s` returns `2 10 0 null` instead of `2 10 0 500`: the projection shows the count as 0, but the join compared NULL.
- Q4 with `JOIN` instead of `LEFT JOIN` returns no rows instead of `2 10 0`.
- The same sub-queries are right when nothing but the projection reads the aggregate: `CROSS JOIN LATERAL (SELECT count(*)::INT cnt FROM c WHERE x = o.x)` returns `1 1`, `2 0`, and Q3 without the join to `s` returns `1 10 1`, `2 10 0`.

Likely cause: the rewrite groups the aggregate by the outer key, so an outer value without input rows has no group. The compensation for `count` (0 instead of NULL) applies in the projection of the level only; filters, ON clauses and later joins read the raw value, and INNER joins drop the row.

### 3. Queries fail with NullPointerException, or lose rows, when the distinct outer values are read by more than one consumer

The rewrite computes the distinct outer values once and shares that cursor between the places that need it (`(Shared)` in the plan). In Q1, Q2 and Q4 a consumer reads the shared cursor before its owner has initialised it (the `circuitBreaker` field of the owner is still null). Q3 has the same plan shape and loses a row instead.

```sql
CREATE TABLE o (id INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO o VALUES (1, 1), (2, 2);
CREATE TABLE a (id INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO a VALUES (10, 1), (11, 2);
CREATE TABLE b (id INT, k INT, x INT, y INT);
INSERT INTO b VALUES (20, 1, 1, 5), (21, 2, 2, 6);
CREATE TABLE c (id INT, y INT);
INSERT INTO c VALUES (30, 5), (31, 6);

-- Q1
SELECT o.id, t.aid, t.bid, t.cid FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT a.id aid, s.id bid, c.id cid FROM a
    JOIN (SELECT id, k, y FROM b WHERE x != o.x) s ON s.k = a.x
    LEFT JOIN c ON c.y = s.y
) t ORDER BY 1, 2, 3, 4;

-- Q2
SELECT o.id, t.aid, t.bid FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT a.id aid, s.id bid FROM a
    JOIN (SELECT id, k, x FROM b WHERE k = o.x OR k = o.id) s ON s.x = a.x
    WHERE s.k = o.x
) t ORDER BY 1, 2, 3;

-- Q3
SELECT o.id, t.cid, t.sid FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT c.id cid, s.id sid FROM c
    LEFT JOIN (
        SELECT n1.id, n1.y FROM b n1 JOIN (SELECT k FROM b WHERE k != o.x) n2 ON n2.k = n1.k
    ) s ON s.y = c.y
) t ORDER BY 1, 2, 3;

-- Q4: Q3 with an INNER join
SELECT o.id, t.cid, t.sid FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT c.id cid, s.id sid FROM c
    JOIN (
        SELECT n1.id, n1.y FROM b n1 JOIN (SELECT k FROM b WHERE k != o.x) n2 ON n2.k = n1.k
    ) s ON s.y = c.y
) t ORDER BY 1, 2, 3;
```

| Query | Expected | Actual |
|---|---|---|
| Q1 | `1 11 21 31`, `2 10 20 30` | `NullPointerException` |
| Q2 | `1 10 20`, `2 11 21` | `NullPointerException` |
| Q3 | `1 30 null`, `1 31 21`, `2 30 20`, `2 31 null` | `1 30 null`, `1 31 21`, `2 30 null`, `2 31 null` |
| Q4 | `1 31 21`, `2 30 20` | `NullPointerException` |

In Q3 no error is raised: the first row of `b` is missing from the joined sub-query, so `2 30 20` comes back as `2 30 null`. Q4 is the same body with an INNER join and fails.

