### 4. Only the first of two INNER-joined correlated sub-queries is tied to the outer row

```sql
CREATE TABLE o (id INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO o VALUES (1, 1), (2, 2);
CREATE TABLE a (id INT, k INT);
INSERT INTO a VALUES (10, 1);
CREATE TABLE b (id INT, k INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO b VALUES (20, 1, 1), (21, 1, 2);
CREATE TABLE c (id INT, k INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO c VALUES (30, 1, 1), (31, 1, 2);

SELECT o.id, t.aid, t.bid, t.cid FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT a.id aid, q.id bid, r.id cid FROM a
    JOIN (SELECT id, k FROM b WHERE x = o.x) q ON q.k = a.k
    JOIN (SELECT id, k FROM c WHERE x = o.x) r ON r.k = q.k
) t ORDER BY 1, 2, 3, 4;
```

Expected: `1 10 20 30`, `2 10 21 31`.

Actual: `1 10 20 30`, `1 10 20 31`, `2 10 21 30`, `2 10 21 31`. The rows of `c` for the other outer row are joined in. The plan scans `c` with no condition on `x`, and the lateral key comes from the first sub-query alone:

```
Hash Join
  condition: t.__qdb_outer_ref__1_x=o.x
    ...
    Hash
        SelectedRecord
            Hash Join Light
              condition: r.k=q.k
                Hash Join Light
                  condition: q.k=a.k
                    ... Frame forward scan on: a
                    Hash
                        SelectedRecord
                            ... Frame forward scan on: b
                Hash
                    ... Frame forward scan on: c
```

With `LEFT JOIN` for the second sub-query the rows are right.

Likely location: `LateralJoinRewriter.pushDownPerSidePush()`, which gives each correlated sub-query its own copy of the outer values and aligns only the first copy with the outer row.

### 5. A correlated UNION ALL sub-query on the slave side of a LEFT JOIN loses the rows of its second branch

```sql
CREATE TABLE o (id INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO o VALUES (1, 1), (2, 2);
CREATE TABLE a (id INT, k INT);
INSERT INTO a VALUES (10, 1), (11, 2);
CREATE TABLE b (id INT, k INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO b VALUES (20, 1, 1), (21, 2, 2);
CREATE TABLE c (id INT, k INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO c VALUES (30, 1, 1), (31, 2, 2);

SELECT o.id, t.aid, t.rid FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT a.id aid, r.id rid FROM a
    LEFT JOIN (
        SELECT id, k FROM b WHERE x = o.x
        UNION ALL
        SELECT id, k FROM c WHERE x = o.x
    ) r ON r.k = a.k
) t ORDER BY 1, 2, 3;
```

Expected: `1 10 20`, `1 10 30`, `1 11 null`, `2 10 null`, `2 11 21`, `2 11 31`.

Actual: `1 10 20`, `1 11 null`, `2 10 null`, `2 11 21`. The rows of `c` (30 and 31) are missing.

- With `JOIN` instead of `LEFT JOIN` the result is right (`1 10 20`, `1 10 30`, `2 11 21`, `2 11 31`).
- The same UNION ALL as the body of the LATERAL sub-query is right.

In the plan both UNION ALL branches read the shared cursor of outer values, so this may be the defect of section 3; it was not traced further.

### 6. Outer columns with the same name from two outer tables are treated as one column in a joined sub-query

```sql
CREATE TABLE o1 (id INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO o1 VALUES (1, 1), (2, 2);
CREATE TABLE o2 (id INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO o2 VALUES (1, 5), (2, 6);
CREATE TABLE a (id INT, k INT);
INSERT INTO a VALUES (10, 1), (11, 2);
CREATE TABLE b (id INT, k INT, x INT, y INT);
INSERT INTO b VALUES (20, 1, 1, 5), (21, 2, 2, 6);

-- Q1
SELECT p.id, t.aid, t.rid FROM o1 p JOIN o2 q ON q.id = p.id CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT a.id aid, r.id rid FROM a
    LEFT JOIN (SELECT id, k FROM b WHERE x = p.x AND y = q.x) r ON r.k = a.k
) t ORDER BY 1, 2, 3;

-- Q2: the same body with an INNER join
SELECT p.id, t.aid, t.rid FROM o1 p JOIN o2 q ON q.id = p.id CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT a.id aid, r.id rid FROM a
    JOIN (SELECT id, k FROM b WHERE x = p.x AND y = q.x) r ON r.k = a.k
) t ORDER BY 1, 2, 3;
```

| Query | Expected | master | PR #7618 branch |
|---|---|---|---|
| Q1 | `1 10 20`, `1 11 null`, `2 10 null`, `2 11 21` | no rows | `1 10 null`, `1 11 null`, `2 10 null`, `2 11 null` |
| Q2 | `1 10 20`, `2 11 21` | no rows | no rows |

`p.x` and `q.x` get the same name in the sub-query's copy of the outer values (`__qdb_outer_ref__1_x`), so `x = p.x AND y = q.x` runs as two conditions on one column. From the plan of Q1 on master:

```
Hash Left Outer Join Light
  condition: r.__qdb_outer_ref__1_x=__qdb_outer_ref__0_x1 and r.k=a.k
    Cross Join
        ... Frame forward scan on: a
        Filter filter: __qdb_outer_ref__0_x=__qdb_outer_ref__0_x1
            GroupBy vectorized: false
              keys: [__qdb_outer_ref__0_x,__qdb_outer_ref__0_x1]
```

The filter, which stands for `p.x = q.x`, removes every outer row here. On the PR branch the plan has no such filter; the LEFT join carries `r.__qdb_outer_ref__1_x=__qdb_outer_ref__0_x1 and r.__qdb_outer_ref__1_x=__qdb_outer_ref__0_x and r.k=a.k` and never matches.

- Renaming one column on the outer side, `JOIN (SELECT id, x x2 FROM o2) q ... AND y = q.x2`, returns the expected rows.
- A body over `b` alone (`SELECT id FROM b WHERE x = p.x AND y = q.x`) is right.

Likely location: the naming of the sub-query's copy of the outer columns (`saveAndRemapOuterToInnerAlias()`), which uses the unqualified column name.

### 7. A window function or LATEST ON above a join to a correlated sub-query runs over the rows of all outer rows

```sql
CREATE TABLE o (id INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO o VALUES (1, 1), (2, 2);
CREATE TABLE a (id INT, k INT, ts TIMESTAMP) TIMESTAMP(ts);
INSERT INTO a VALUES (10, 1, '2024-01-01T00:00:00.000000Z'), (11, 2, '2024-01-01T00:00:01.000000Z');
CREATE TABLE b (id INT, k INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO b VALUES (20, 1, 1), (21, 2, 1), (22, 1, 2), (23, 2, 2);

-- Q1: row_number()
SELECT o.id, t.bid, t.rn FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT bid, row_number() OVER (ORDER BY bid) rn FROM (
        SELECT q.id bid FROM a JOIN (SELECT id, k FROM b WHERE x = o.x) q ON q.k = a.k
    )
) t ORDER BY 1, 2;

-- Q2: count(*) OVER ()
SELECT o.id, t.bid, t.c FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT bid, count(*) OVER () c FROM (
        SELECT q.id bid FROM a JOIN (SELECT id, k FROM b WHERE x = o.x) q ON q.k = a.k
    )
) t ORDER BY 1, 2;

-- Q3: LATEST ON
SELECT o.id, t.aid, t.bid FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT aid, bid FROM (
        (SELECT a.id aid, a.k ak, q.id bid, a.ts FROM a JOIN (SELECT id, k FROM b WHERE x = o.x) q ON q.k = a.k) TIMESTAMP(ts)
    ) LATEST ON ts PARTITION BY ak
) t ORDER BY 1, 2, 3;
```

| Query | Expected | Actual |
|---|---|---|
| Q1 | `1 20 1`, `1 21 2`, `2 22 1`, `2 23 2` | `1 20 1`, `1 21 2`, `2 22 3`, `2 23 4` |
| Q2 | `1 20 2`, `1 21 2`, `2 22 2`, `2 23 2` | `1 20 4`, `1 21 4`, `2 22 4`, `2 23 4` |
| Q3 | `1 10 20`, `1 11 21`, `2 10 22`, `2 11 23` | two of the four rows |

Q3 returns `2 10 22`, `2 11 23` on master and `1 10 20`, `1 11 21` on the PR branch: the rows of the two outer rows share one partition and tie on `ts`.

- Over a single-table sub-query (`FROM (SELECT id bid FROM b WHERE x = o.x)`) Q1 is right.
- With the correlation in the WHERE clause of a plain join (`FROM a JOIN b ON b.k = a.k WHERE b.x = o.x`) Q1 is right.
- With `RIGHT JOIN` to the correlated sub-query Q1 returns the same wrong numbering.

The window function, and the `row_number()` that LATEST ON becomes, get no partition on the outer key in this shape.

### 8. LIMIT in the sub-query orders by the wrong column, or drops the ordering that a lower layer needs

```sql
CREATE TABLE o (id INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO o VALUES (1, 1), (2, 2);
CREATE TABLE c (v INT, x INT, w INT, g INT);
INSERT INTO c VALUES (301, 1, 5, 1), (302, 1, 3, 1), (303, 1, 7, 2), (304, 2, 9, 1), (305, 2, 1, 2), (306, 2, 4, 2), (307, 1, 1, 2);

-- Q1: ORDER BY names a recomputed output column
SELECT o.id, t.v, t.w FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT -v AS v, w FROM (SELECT v, w FROM c WHERE x = o.x) ORDER BY v LIMIT 2
) t ORDER BY 1, 2;

-- Q2: ORDER BY names a renamed output column
SELECT o.id, t.v, t.w FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT w AS v, v AS w FROM (SELECT v, w FROM c WHERE x = o.x) ORDER BY v LIMIT 2
) t ORDER BY 1, 2;

-- Q3: a LIMIT above an ORDER BY ... LIMIT
SELECT o.id, t.v, t.w FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT v, w FROM (
        SELECT v, w FROM (SELECT v, w FROM c WHERE x = o.x) ORDER BY w LIMIT 2
    ) LIMIT 1000
) t ORDER BY 1, 2;

-- Q4: a LIMIT above first() over an ordered sub-query
SELECT o.id, t.g, t.w FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT g, w FROM (
        SELECT g, first(w) w FROM (
            SELECT g, w FROM (SELECT g, w FROM c WHERE x = o.x) ORDER BY w DESC
        ) GROUP BY g
    ) LIMIT 1000
) t ORDER BY 1, 2;

-- Q5: a LIMIT above row_number() OVER () over an ordered sub-query
SELECT o.id, t.v, t.w, t.rn FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT v, w, rn FROM (
        SELECT v, w, row_number() OVER () rn FROM (
            SELECT v, w FROM (SELECT v, w FROM c WHERE x = o.x) ORDER BY w
        )
    ) LIMIT 1000
) t ORDER BY 1, 2;
```

| Query | Expected | Actual |
|---|---|---|
| Q1 | `1 -307 1`, `1 -303 7`, `2 -306 4`, `2 -305 1` | `1 -302 3`, `1 -301 5`, `2 -305 1`, `2 -304 9` |
| Q2 | `1 1 307`, `1 3 302`, `2 1 305`, `2 4 306` | `1 3 302`, `1 5 301`, `2 1 305`, `2 9 304` |
| Q3 | `1 302 3`, `1 307 1`, `2 305 1`, `2 306 4` | `1 301 5`, `1 302 3`, `2 304 9`, `2 305 1` |
| Q4 | `1 1 5`, `1 2 7`, `2 1 9`, `2 2 4` | `1 1 5`, `1 2 7`, `2 1 9`, `2 2 1` |
| Q5 | `1 301 5 3`, `1 302 3 2`, `1 303 7 4`, `1 307 1 1`, `2 304 9 3`, `2 305 1 1`, `2 306 4 2` | `1 301 5 1`, `1 302 3 2`, `1 303 7 3`, `1 307 1 4`, `2 304 9 1`, `2 305 1 2`, `2 306 4 3` |

- Q1 and Q2 order by the input column `v`, not by the output column that `ORDER BY v` names.
- In Q3 the outer `LIMIT 1000` takes the `ORDER BY w` of the inner `LIMIT 2`, which then keeps the first two rows in scan order.
- In Q4 and Q5 the outer `LIMIT 1000` takes the ORDER BY from below `first()` and `row_number() OVER ()`, which then see the rows in scan order. Without the outer LIMIT, Q4 is right.
- A single `SELECT v, w FROM (...) ORDER BY w LIMIT 2` is right.

Likely location: `LateralJoinRewriter.compensateLimit()`. It turns a LIMIT into `row_number() OVER (PARTITION BY <outer key> ORDER BY ...)`. It looks for the ORDER BY from the LIMIT layer down to the first table or join level, resolves the names against the input of the LIMIT layer, and clears the ORDER BY it took.

### 9. Internal columns appear in `t.*`, `SELECT *` and `CREATE TABLE AS`

```sql
CREATE TABLE o (id INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO o VALUES (1, 1), (2, 2);
CREATE TABLE b (bid INT, x INT, ts TIMESTAMP) TIMESTAMP(ts);
INSERT INTO b VALUES (20, 1, '2024-01-01T00:00:00.000000Z'), (21, 1, '2024-01-01T00:00:01.000000Z'), (22, 2, '2024-01-01T00:00:02.000000Z');

