    SELECT o.ts ots, o.ts::LONG ol, o.ts + 1 ts1, v FROM n WHERE tn = o.ts
) t ORDER BY 1;

-- Q2: the values of the outer column itself
SELECT id, ts ots, ts::LONG ol, ts + 1 ts1 FROM o ORDER BY 1;
```

| Column | Expected (Q2) | Actual (Q1) |
|---|---|---|
| type of `ots`, `ts1` | `TIMESTAMP` | `TIMESTAMP_NS` |
| `ots` | `2024-01-01T00:00:01.000000Z` | `2024-01-01T00:00:01.000000000Z` |
| `ol` | `1704067201000000` | `1704067201000000000` |
| `ts1` | `2024-01-01T00:00:01.000001Z` | `2024-01-01T00:00:01.000000001Z` |

(Values of outer row 1; outer row 2 differs the same way.) `o.ts::LONG` is off by a factor of 1,000, and `o.ts + 1` adds a nanosecond instead of a microsecond.

Likely cause: the rewrite removes the join to the outer values when WHERE equates the outer column with an inner column, and reads the inner column in place of the outer one. That is only sound when both have the same type.

