### RIGHT and FULL joins in the body, and PR #7618

RIGHT and FULL joins in the body are a class of their own and are not part of the list above. On master such a query compiles, and its result depends on the data: it is right only when the RIGHT or FULL join has no unmatched row to keep. #7723, #7724 and #7726 describe the wrong rows.

PR #7618 (checked at 0dd219583d) changes one thing here. A body no longer compiles when the first reader of an outer column is the RIGHT or FULL join itself, or a LEFT join after it. "Reader" means the join's ON clause, or a correlated sub-query on its slave side. Such a query fails with `Invalid table name or alias` or with `Invalid column: __qdb_outer_ref__0_<column>`. That removes the wrong rows of these shapes, and it also rejects the queries that master answered correctly. A correlation in the body's WHERE clause, and an INNER join to a correlated sub-query after the RIGHT or FULL join, still compile. An example with data on which master is right:

```sql
CREATE TABLE o (id INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO o VALUES (1, 1), (2, 2);
CREATE TABLE a (id INT, k INT);
INSERT INTO a VALUES (10, 1), (11, 2);
CREATE TABLE b (id INT, k INT, x INT);
INSERT INTO b VALUES (20, 1, 1), (21, 2, 2);

-- Q1: a sub-query with a != correlation LEFT-joined after a FULL JOIN
SELECT o.id, t.aid, t.cid, t.sid FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT a.id aid, c.id cid, s.id sid FROM a
    FULL JOIN b c ON c.k = a.k
    LEFT JOIN (SELECT id, k FROM b WHERE x != o.x) s ON s.k = a.k
) t ORDER BY 1, 2, 3, 4;

-- Q2: the FULL JOIN's slave is a correlated sub-query
SELECT o.id, t.aid, t.cid FROM o CROSS JOIN LATERAL (
    SELECT a.id aid, c.id cid FROM a
    FULL JOIN (SELECT id, k FROM b WHERE x = o.x) c ON c.k = a.k
) t ORDER BY 1, 2, 3;
```

| Query | master | PR #7618 branch |
|---|---|---|
| Q1 | `1 10 20 null`, `1 11 21 21`, `2 10 20 20`, `2 11 21 null` (correct for this data) | `[0] Invalid column: __qdb_outer_ref__0_x` |
| Q2 | `1 10 20`, `1 11 null`, `2 10 null`, `2 11 21` (correct for this data) | `Invalid table name or alias`, positioned at `o.x` |

Further observations on the PR branch:

- The queries of #7724 B1 and B2 and of #7723 Q1 and Q3 fail to compile there. #7723 Q4 and #7724 B3 and B4 (the reader comes before the RIGHT or FULL join, or the correlated sub-query is the FROM source) still return the wrong rows that master returns.
- The failed compilation of Q1 leaves 49,244 bytes of `NATIVE_ROSTI` allocated on every attempt. Other shapes of this class leave `NATIVE_OFFLOAD` or `NATIVE_JOIN_MAP` memory behind.
- A few shapes fail with another error, for example `Ambiguous column [name=y]` at position 0, or `UnsupportedOperationException` (the stack of section 15).

Correct support for this class, by decorrelating on the preserved side of the RIGHT or FULL join as #7723 and #7724 suggest, is follow-up work.

### Related issues

These existing issues cover the other findings of the same review; none of them is repeated above.

- #7725: a join to a correlated sub-query loses its ON condition when the body reads a second outer column. Section 1 is the same replacement on the LATERAL join's own ON clause. Another instance of #7725: `a LEFT JOIN (SELECT id, k, y FROM b WHERE y = o.x) r ON r.y = a.y WHERE r.k = o.k` returns rows with `r.y != a.y`.
- #7731: other correlated conditions are ignored when every outer column has an equality. It also applies inside a joined sub-query: `LEFT JOIN (SELECT id, k FROM b WHERE x = o.x AND k < o.x + 1) r ON r.k = a.k` ignores `k < o.x + 1`, on master and on the PR #7618 branch.
- #7723: the same row loss when the ON clause of an INNER join before a FULL join has a condition on outer columns only (`a JOIN b ON b.k = a.k AND o.x = 1 FULL JOIN c ON c.k = a.k`): the outer rows that fail the condition get no rows at all, where the FULL join keeps the rows of `c` for them.
- #7724: a correlated FROM sub-query on the master side of a RIGHT or FULL join. The same happens one level down, when that pair is itself a joined sub-query: `a LEFT JOIN (SELECT n2.id, n2.k FROM (SELECT id, k FROM b WHERE x = o.x) n1 RIGHT JOIN b n2 ON n2.id = n1.id) r ON r.k = a.k`.
- #7726: rows of other outer rows attached to the outer row with a NULL key after a RIGHT or FULL join.
- #7732: unmatched rows of a LEFT join lost for a NULL outer key when WHERE equates the outer column with the join's slave; also with a FULL join earlier in the body.
- #7729: an INNER or RIGHT join to a sub-query whose ON clause reads an outer column fails to compile. When that sub-query has a correlated join of its own, the message names an inner alias instead: `Invalid column: n1.k`.
- #7694, #7695, #7727, #7728, #7730, #7737, #7785: other LATERAL shapes that fail to compile or return wrong rows. #7695 is the closest to section 10.
- #7700: the `wtf?` assertion in `generateSelectChoose()` and the hash-join map it leaks, for a query without LATERAL (see section 13).

