### What triggers it

- A process stop between the data write and the `_txn` write: kill -9, the OOM killer, a JVM crash, a container killed after its grace period, a host crash.
- An exception in the same window, without a crash. With `cairo.commit.mode=sync`, an `msync` failure in `commit00` suspends the table, and `ALTER TABLE ... RESUME WAL`, the usual recovery step, replays the damaged lag. Other exceptions in the window likely behave the same; only `msync` was tested.
- A clean shutdown does not: the apply job checks for termination between transactions, so the commit in flight writes `_txn`. This comes from reading the code, not from a test. A shutdown that times out into SIGKILL is a process stop.
- Power loss was not tested. In the default `nosync` commit mode, QuestDB makes no consistency promise after power loss in any case.

### Where it happens

Both paths change the last partition's files before [`commit00`](https://github.com/questdb/questdb/blob/d3b877ccc9591d23121b4b62c41c9f959818d9f7/core/src/main/java/io/questdb/cairo/TableWriter.java#L5586) writes `_txn` (links point to master, d3b877ccc9):

1. In-place append. When every row the commit puts into the last partition sorts at or after its committed maximum, `processO3Block` appends through the writer's column memories: [`setAppendPosition(srcDataMax, false)`](https://github.com/questdb/questdb/blob/d3b877ccc9591d23121b4b62c41c9f959818d9f7/core/src/main/java/io/questdb/cairo/TableWriter.java#L10279) moves the append position back to the committed row count, and [`O3OpenColumnJob.appendLastPartition`](https://github.com/questdb/questdb/blob/d3b877ccc9591d23121b4b62c41c9f959818d9f7/core/src/main/java/io/questdb/cairo/TableWriter.java#L10332) writes the sorted merge of lag and new rows from there, over the lag region. When the new transaction's rows sort before some lag rows, which is the usual reason the lag exists, the region no longer holds the lag.
2. Trim. In `o3ConsumePartitionUpdateSink`, [`closeActivePartition(srcDataNewPartitionSize)`](https://github.com/questdb/questdb/blob/d3b877ccc9591d23121b4b62c41c9f959818d9f7/core/src/main/java/io/questdb/cairo/TableWriter.java#L8917) closes the last partition after an append when a new partition becomes the last one, and the split branch calls [`closeActivePartition(committedLastPartitionSize)`](https://github.com/questdb/questdb/blob/d3b877ccc9591d23121b4b62c41c9f959818d9f7/core/src/main/java/io/questdb/cairo/TableWriter.java#L8976). Both truncate the column files to `ceilPageSize(rows * width)` and cut the lag rows beyond that page. The tests cover the first; the split branch is from reading the code.

The control passes because a commit that moves the lag into a new partition writes through that partition's own files, and `openPartition` closes the old partition at its append offset, which ends after the lag.

### Why recovery does not catch it

- The lag checksum in `_txn` ([`calculateTxnLagChecksum`](https://github.com/questdb/questdb/blob/d3b877ccc9591d23121b4b62c41c9f959818d9f7/core/src/main/java/io/questdb/cairo/TxWriter.java#L689)) covers counts, timestamps and the txn, not the data.
- The restarted writer positions the last partition at the committed row count plus the lag row count. After a trim, that maps past the end of the file, so the cut rows read as zeros.
- The re-apply accepts the zero timestamps.
- [`TableSnapshotRestore`](https://github.com/questdb/questdb/blob/d3b877ccc9591d23121b4b62c41c9f959818d9f7/core/src/main/java/io/questdb/cairo/TableSnapshotRestore.java#L466) resets the lag on restore for this reason ("WAL Lag values is not strictly append-only data structures, it can be overwritten while the snapshot was copied"), so a restored table re-applies the lag transactions from WAL. Crash recovery has no equivalent.

### Exposure

The apply job persists the lag whenever it exceeds `cairo.wal.apply.table.time.quota` (1 s by default) or stops, which is routine under sustained out-of-order ingestion. The window of the in-place append covers the whole data write of the consuming commit plus `commit00`, which in `sync` mode includes an `msync` of every column.

### Possible directions (not verified)

- Trim paths: never trim below the rows the `_txn` on disk can still reference, that is the pre-commit row count plus the lag row count, as #7644 does for its own trim. `processWalCommitFinishApply` resets the lag count only after the O3 commit finishes.
- In-place append: either keep the lag region intact until `_txn` lands, or write `_txn` with the lag reset before the commit overwrites the region, so a restart re-applies the lag transactions from WAL, as `TableSnapshotRestore` does. The second option needs the WAL segments of those transactions to stay on disk until then.

