body_text:
DATE covers far more years than TIMESTAMP, which ends in year 294247. A later DATE converts to an unrelated timestamp without an error, so casts are wrong and DATE-to-TIMESTAMP comparisons return wrong rows.

### To reproduce

```sql
CREATE TABLE d (k INT, v DATE);
INSERT INTO d VALUES (1, 10_000_000_000_000_000::DATE), (2, '2024-01-01'::DATE);
SELECT k, v, v::TIMESTAMP FROM d;
SELECT k FROM d WHERE v > '2000-01-01T00:00:00.000000Z'::TIMESTAMP;
```

Actual: row 1 (`318857-05-20T17:46:40.000Z`) converts to `-265697-05-04T09:44:50.448384Z`, so the WHERE returns only row 2.
Expected: an error (or NULL), never a different valid timestamp. The test expects the error that `'2300-01-01'::TIMESTAMP::TIMESTAMP_NS` already gives.

Also: DATE to TIMESTAMP_NS wraps from year 2263 (`'2300-01-01'::DATE::TIMESTAMP_NS` gives year 1715). Related: #7389.

Likely cause: `MicrosTimestampDriver.fromDate` and `NanosTimestampDriver.fromDate` multiply without an overflow check.

Reproduced on master 6c63556431.

<details>
<summary>Failing test</summary>

Add to `core/src/test/java/io/questdb/test/griffin/engine/functions/cast/CastTest.java`:

```java
@Test
public void testDateToTimestampOutOfRange() throws Exception {
    // year 318857 fits a DATE (milliseconds) but not a TIMESTAMP (microseconds, up to year 294247);
    // the conversion must not wrap to a valid-looking timestamp. TIMESTAMP to TIMESTAMP_NS
    // already fails this way: '2300-01-01'::TIMESTAMP::TIMESTAMP_NS
    assertQuery("SELECT v::TIMESTAMP FROM d")
            .ddl("""
                    CREATE TABLE d AS (
                        SELECT 10_000_000_000_000_000::DATE v FROM long_sequence(1)
                    )
                    """)
            .fails(-1, "inconvertible value");
}
```

On master it fails with: `java.lang.AssertionError: SQL statement should have failed`

</details>

