There is no LONG256 version of `first()` or `last()`, so the call quietly uses the LONG version and returns the low 64 bits of the value as a LONG.

### To reproduce

```sql
CREATE TABLE g (v LONG256, ts TIMESTAMP) TIMESTAMP(ts);
INSERT INTO g VALUES
  (0x0100000000000000000000000000000005, '2024-01-01T00:00:00.000000Z'),
  (0xffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffffff, '2024-01-01T00:00:01.000000Z');
SELECT first(v), last(v), typeOf(last(v)) FROM g;
```

Actual: `5`, `-1`, `LONG`.
Expected: the two LONG256 values, or a "no matching function" error rather than a different number.

Also: `first_not_null()`, `last_not_null()`, and comparisons of a LONG with a LONG256 constant use the same low 64 bits.

Reproduced on master 6c63556431.

