body_text:
After `ALTER COLUMN ... TYPE` on a base table, a full refresh of a dependent materialized view writes wrong values and reports the view `valid`.

### To reproduce

```sql
CREATE TABLE base_price (sym VARCHAR, at TIMESTAMP, ts TIMESTAMP) TIMESTAMP(ts) PARTITION BY DAY WAL;
CREATE MATERIALIZED VIEW price_1h AS (SELECT sym, last(at) AS at, ts FROM base_price SAMPLE BY 1h) PARTITION BY DAY;
INSERT INTO base_price VALUES ('gbpusd', '2024-09-10T12:01', '2024-09-10T12:01');
-- after the view refreshes:
ALTER TABLE base_price ALTER COLUMN at TYPE VARCHAR;
REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW price_1h FULL;
-- after the refresh:
SELECT * FROM price_1h;
```

Actual: `at` is `56663-10-20T16:40:00.000000Z`, and `materialized_views()` reports `valid`.
Expected: `2024-09-10T12:01:00.000000Z`.

Also: the same with `TYPE SYMBOL`. If the server restarts between the ALTER and the refresh, the view gets NULL instead, still `valid`.

Likely cause: `MatViewRefreshJob` reuses its cached row copier while the view table's metadata version is unchanged, and the base table's ALTER does not change it.

Reproduced on master 6c63556431.

<details>
<summary>Failing test</summary>

Add to `core/src/test/java/io/questdb/test/cairo/mv/MatViewTest.java`:

```java
    @Test
    public void testFullRefreshAfterBaseColumnTypeChange() throws Exception {
        assertMemoryLeak(() -> {
            execute("CREATE TABLE base_price (sym VARCHAR, at TIMESTAMP, ts TIMESTAMP) TIMESTAMP(ts) PARTITION BY DAY WAL");
            createMatView("SELECT sym, last(at) AS at, ts FROM base_price SAMPLE BY 1h");
            execute("INSERT INTO base_price VALUES ('gbpusd', '2024-09-10T12:01', '2024-09-10T12:01')");
            drainQueues();

            execute("ALTER TABLE base_price ALTER COLUMN at TYPE VARCHAR");
            drainQueues();
            execute("REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW price_1h FULL");
            drainQueues();

            // the refreshed view must hold what its query yields: the text '2024-09-10T12:01:00.000000Z'
            // converted to the view's TIMESTAMP column
            assertQuery("SELECT view_status FROM materialized_views")
                    .noRandomAccess()
                    .noLeakCheck()
                    .returns("view_status\nvalid\n");
            assertQuery("price_1h")
                    .timestamp("ts")
                    .expectSize()
                    .noLeakCheck()
                    .returns("""
                            sym\tat\tts
                            gbpusd\t2024-09-10T12:01:00.000000Z\t2024-09-10T12:00:00.000000Z
                            """);
        });
    }
```

On master it fails with: `expected:<...gbpusd 2024-09-10T12:01:00.000000Z ...> but was:<...gbpusd 56663-10-20T16:40:00.000000Z ...>`
</details>

