body_text:
With binary results, QuestDB shifts TIMESTAMP and DATE to PostgreSQL's 2000-01-01 epoch without an overflow check. The smallest TIMESTAMP arrives as a far-future value, and the DATE extremes arrive next to 1970. Text format and HTTP are correct.

### To reproduce

```sql
CREATE TABLE t (k VARCHAR, ts TIMESTAMP, d DATE);
INSERT INTO t VALUES
    ('min', -9223372036854775807::TIMESTAMP, -9223372036854775807::DATE),
    ('max', 9223372036854775807::TIMESTAMP, 9223372036854775807::DATE);
SELECT k, ts, d FROM t;   -- JDBC with binaryTransfer=true&prepareThreshold=-1
```

Actual: `min` reads `294247-01-10 04:00:54.775809` and `1970-01-01 00:00:00.001`; `max` reads `294247-01-10 04:00:54.775807` and `1969-12-31 23:59:59.999`.
Expected: min before 1970 and max after it, for example clamped, or sent as `-infinity` / `infinity` as PostgreSQL does.

Likely cause: `PGPipelineEntry.outColBinTimestamp` and `outColBinDate` subtract the epoch offset without an overflow check.

Reproduced on master 6c63556431.

<details>
<summary>Failing test</summary>

Add to `core/src/test/java/io/questdb/test/cutlass/pgwire/PGJobContextTest.java`:

```java
@Test
public void testBinaryTimestampAndDateExtremesKeepTheirOrder() throws Exception {
    // Binary TIMESTAMP counts microseconds from 2000-01-01. Shifting QuestDB's extremes to that
    // epoch must not wrap them around to the other end of the range.
    assertWithPgServerExtendedBinaryOnly((connection, _, _, _) -> {
        try (Statement stmt = connection.createStatement()) {
            stmt.execute("CREATE TABLE t (k VARCHAR, ts TIMESTAMP, d DATE)");
            stmt.execute("""
                    INSERT INTO t VALUES
                        ('min', -9223372036854775807::TIMESTAMP, -9223372036854775807::DATE),
                        ('epoch', 0::TIMESTAMP, 0::DATE),
                        ('max', 9223372036854775807::TIMESTAMP, 9223372036854775807::DATE)""");
        }
        try (
                PreparedStatement ps = connection.prepareStatement("SELECT ts, d FROM t");
                ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()
        ) {
            final Timestamp[][] v = new Timestamp[3][];
            for (int row = 0; row < 3; row++) {
                assertTrue(rs.next());
                v[row] = new Timestamp[]{rs.getTimestamp(1), rs.getTimestamp(2)};
            }
            final String read = "min=" + Arrays.toString(v[0]) + ", epoch=" + Arrays.toString(v[1]) + ", max=" + Arrays.toString(v[2]);
            for (int col = 0; col < 2; col++) {
                final String type = col == 0 ? "TIMESTAMP" : "DATE";
                assertTrue(type + " min must read before the epoch; read " + read, v[0][col].before(v[1][col]));
                assertTrue(type + " max must read after the epoch; read " + read, v[2][col].after(v[1][col]));
            }
        }
    });
}
```

On master it fails with: `java.lang.AssertionError: TIMESTAMP min must read before the epoch; read min=[294247-01-10 04:00:54.775809, 1970-01-01 00:00:00.001], epoch=[1970-01-01 00:00:00.0, 1970-01-01 00:00:00.0], max=[294247-01-10 04:00:54.775807, 1969-12-31 23:59:59.999]`

</details>

