Writing `"ab"` to a CHAR column over QWP silently stores `a`. SQL `INSERT`, and ILP over TCP and HTTP, refuse such a value.

### To reproduce

```sql
CREATE TABLE t (col CHAR, ts TIMESTAMP) TIMESTAMP(ts) PARTITION BY DAY WAL;
```

Send with the Java client (`ws::`): `table("t").stringColumn("col", "ab").at(...)`.

Actual: the row is stored with `col` = `a`.
Expected: the value is refused.

Also: CSV import (`/imp`, `COPY`) stores the first character too. ILP over UDP does so by design (`LineUdpParserImplTest.testCharStringIsProvided`), and ILP over TCP and HTTP only with `line.tcp.undocumented.string.to.char.cast.allowed=true`.

Reproduced on master 6c63556431.

<details>
<summary>Failing test</summary>

Add to `core/src/test/java/io/questdb/test/cutlass/qwp/e2e/QwpWebSocketTypeConversionE2ETest.java`
(imports `io.questdb.client.LineSenderServerException`, `io.questdb.client.SenderError`,
`java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture`):

```java
@Test
public void testStringToCharColumnRefusesLongerString() throws Exception {
    // SQL INSERT, ILP over TCP and ILP over HTTP refuse "ab" for a CHAR column;
    // QWP must not store its first character either
    runInContext((port) -> {
        execute("CREATE TABLE tc_str2_char (col CHAR, ts TIMESTAMP) TIMESTAMP(ts) PARTITION BY DAY WAL");

        final CompletableFuture<SenderError> terminalFut = new CompletableFuture<>();
        final QwpWebSocketSender sender = connectWs(port, err -> {
            if (err.getAppliedPolicy() == SenderError.Policy.TERMINAL) {
                terminalFut.complete(err);
            }
        }, 1);
        try {
            sender.table("tc_str2_char")
                    .stringColumn("col", "ab")
                    .at(1_000_000_000_000L, ChronoUnit.MICROS);
            sender.flush();
        } catch (LineSenderServerException ignored) {
            // a refusal of the batch may surface here
        } finally {
            assertRejectionTerminalOnClose(sender, terminalFut, null);
        }

        drainWalQueue();
        assertQuery("SELECT col FROM tc_str2_char")
                .noLeakCheck()
                .expectSize()
                .returns("col\n");
    });
}
```

On master it fails with an `AssertionError`: the table holds one row, `a`, where none is expected.
</details>

