### Verified on

Master commit `77c6cf2d6b` (9 October 2026), Linux 7.1 x86_64, ext4, JDK 25. The POSTING index first shipped in
9.4.0; not run on a release.

### Suggested fix

Not tried. By reading: the out-of-order commit updates a partition's POSTING index in `O3CopyJob.updateIndex()`,
which opens a pooled index writer with `PostingIndexWriter.openFromO3Context()` and closes it with
`Misc.free(indexWriter)`. That open does not run the chain recovery walk (no `setCurrentTableTxn()`), so after a
crash the abandoned head entry left above the committed txn is found only later by `rollbackConditionally()` /
`rollbackToMaxValue()`, which record the superseded files in the writer's pending purges. `close()` then drops them
in `releasePendingPurges()`, so nobody ever deletes those files. The Parquet out-of-order path already hands such
purges to the table writer (`O3PartitionJob` calls `TableWriter.deferParquetPostingSealPurges()`); doing the same in
`O3CopyJob.updateIndex()` before the writer is freed, with the partition context set, should cover the
before-commit window. The after-commit window needs the published purges to survive a crash, or a start-up scan
that finds superseded generations no chain entry names.

