Put the file under `core/src/test/java/io/questdb/test/cairo/` and run, from the repository root:

```
mvn -B test -pl core -Dtest.include="%regex[.*SyncAlterColumnTypeDurabilityTest.*]" -DfailIfNoTests=false -Dsurefire.failIfNoSpecifiedTests=false
```

### Expected behaviour

In SYNC mode, by the time the `ALTER` returns, the new column files and their directory entries are on disk, flushed
before `_txn` and `_meta` name them. After a power loss the table reads back the converted values.

### Actual behaviour

Both tests (non-WAL and WAL) fail:

```
java.lang.AssertionError: after a power loss the table does not read back; files never flushed:
[x~/2024-01-01/v.d.1 (name and content not flushed), x~/2024-01-02/v.d.1 (name and content not flushed),
x~/2024-01-03/v.d.1 (name not flushed)];
error: [2] could not open, file does not exist: <dbRoot>/x~/2024-01-01/v.d.1
```

An `strace` of the same run, matching each `msync` to its mapping, shows the same thing at the system call level:
after the new files are created, no `fsync`, `fdatasync`, `msync`, `sync_file_range` or `syncfs` reaches `v.d.1` in
the first two partitions or any partition directory. Only the last partition's `v.d.1` content is flushed.

### Verified on

Master `77c6cf2d6b`, 9 October 2026, Linux 7.1 x86_64, ext4. ALTER COLUMN TYPE was added in 8.0.0, and its
conversion code has never flushed what it writes, so every release since 8.0.0 is likely affected (not run on a
release build).

### Suggested fix

In `core/src/main/java/io/questdb/griffin/ConvertOperatorImpl.java`, when the commit mode is not NOSYNC:

- in `cthConvertPartitionHandler`, `fsync` the destination files after `ColumnTypeConverter.convertColumn` succeeds,
  before `closeFds`;
- in `convertColumn0`, after `openColumnsRW` creates a partition's new files, `fsync` that partition directory.

Tried as a scratch change: with these two the test passes. On Linux an `fsync` after the mapping is gone still
writes its pages. On Windows, writes made through a mapping need `FlushViewOfFile` before the file is flushed, so a
portable fix should also `msync` the mapping in `ColumnTypeConverter` before it is unmapped (not tried).

The Javadoc of `TableWriter.commitPendingParquetToNativeConversions` says the final commit's `syncColumns` makes the
conversion output durable. It covers only the columns the writer has open in the last partition.

### Additional context

Found while working on questdb#7411 (adaptive commit mode). Related: #1857 (SYNC mode does not `msync` all written
data). The same kind of gap affects ALTER COLUMN TYPE to SYMBOL and ALTER COLUMN ... ADD INDEX, reported separately.

