### Expected behaviour

A SYNC commit is durable when it returns, without a synchronous flush for every page filled between commits, for
example with one flush per written column file at commit time.

### Actual behaviour

Four alternating runs per mode (milliseconds; SYNC page-release flush time in brackets):

| Ingest of 2,000,000 rows | NOSYNC | SYNC | SYNC / NOSYNC | Page-release `MS_SYNC` calls per ingest |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| WAL writer, commit every 500,000 rows | 86-132 | 1,954-2,188 (1,300-1,445) | 17-25x | 113 |
| `INSERT ... SELECT` | 139-188 | 1,892-1,995 (1,423-1,503) | 10-14x | 113 |

Under NOSYNC the instrument sees no synchronous `msync`. Under SYNC the commit's own flushes (49 and 25 calls per
ingest) took 529-607 ms and 292-316 ms. The assertion:

```
java.lang.AssertionError: synchronous msync calls on WAL page release under SYNC, 4 x 2 ingests of 2000000 rows expected:<0> but was:<904>
```

The machine was shared with other test runs (load average 5.9-6.5 on 24 cores), so absolute times vary; the runs
were interleaved so both modes saw the same load.

### Verified on

Master commit `77c6cf2d6b` (9 October 2026), Linux 7.1 x86_64, ext4 on a Samsung 990 PRO NVMe disk, JDK 25. The
page-release flush has been in `MemoryPMARImpl.release()` since 7.1.2 (from the code history, not run on that
release).

### Suggested fix

Not tried. `MemoryPMARImpl.release()` calls `ff.msync(address, getPageSize(), commitMode == CommitMode.ASYNC)` in
every mode except NOSYNC, so under SYNC each released page is flushed synchronously. The page flush cannot simply be
dropped: `WalWriter.syncIfRequired()` flushes only each column's current page (`MemoryPMARImpl.sync()`), so the
release flush is what makes the earlier pages durable today. On Linux, the release could use `MS_ASYNC` (start
write-back without waiting) and the SYNC commit could `fdatasync` each column file it wrote, which trades one wait
per page for one wait per file per commit. That needs a crash test in SYNC showing the commit is still durable, and
separate checks on macOS and Windows, where `msync`/`MS_ASYNC` semantics differ.

### Additional context

Found while working on questdb#7411. Related: #1857 (still open) reported that SYNC did not flush pages on release;
the flush was added in 7.1.2 (#3304).

