body_text:
A FLOAT just above the largest INT is not refused when it is inserted into an INT column: it is clamped to 2147483647 and stored. The opposite edge is refused, as expected. FLOAT and DOUBLE into a LONG column behave the same at the top of LONG's range.

### To reproduce

```sql
CREATE TABLE f (v FLOAT);
INSERT INTO f VALUES (2147483647.0);  -- as a FLOAT this is 2147483648.0, one above the largest INT
CREATE TABLE i (v INT);
INSERT INTO i SELECT v FROM f;
SELECT v FROM i;
```

Actual: the INSERT succeeds and `i` holds `2147483647`.
Expected: the INSERT fails with `inconvertible value`, as it does for `-2147483647.0`, which as a FLOAT is -2147483648.0.

Also: `9223372036854775807.0` in a FLOAT or a DOUBLE column (2^63, one above the largest LONG) is stored in a LONG column as `9223372036854775807`. DOUBLE into INT is refused correctly.

Likely cause: `SqlUtil.implicitCastFloatAsInt`, `implicitCastFloatAsLong` and `implicitCastDoubleAsLong` check `value <= Integer.MAX_VALUE` (or `Long.MAX_VALUE`). The bound is converted to the floating-point type first and rounds up to 2^31 (2^63), so that value passes the check and the cast clamps it.

Reproduced on master 77c6cf2d6b.

<details>
<summary>Failing test</summary>

Add to `core/src/test/java/io/questdb/test/griffin/InsertCastTest.java`:

```java
@Test
public void testFloatAndDoubleJustAboveRangeAreRefused() throws Exception {
    assertMemoryLeak(() -> {
        execute("CREATE TABLE f (v FLOAT)");
        execute("CREATE TABLE d (v DOUBLE)");
        execute("CREATE TABLE i (v INT)");
        execute("CREATE TABLE l (v LONG)");
        // as a FLOAT, 2147483647.0 rounds up to 2^31, one above the largest INT
        execute("INSERT INTO f VALUES (2147483647.0)");
        assertException("INSERT INTO i SELECT v FROM f", 0, "inconvertible value");
        // as a FLOAT or a DOUBLE, 9223372036854775807.0 rounds up to 2^63, one above the largest LONG
        execute("TRUNCATE TABLE f");
        execute("INSERT INTO f VALUES (9223372036854775807.0)");
        execute("INSERT INTO d VALUES (9223372036854775807.0)");
        assertException("INSERT INTO l SELECT v FROM f", 0, "inconvertible value");
        assertException("INSERT INTO l SELECT v FROM d", 0, "inconvertible value");
    });
}
```

On master it fails at the first `assertException` with `AssertionError: SQL statement should have failed`.

</details>

