body_text:
A WHERE filter that compares a TIMESTAMP_NS column with a timestamp literal in the year 2262 ignores the
literal's sub-microsecond digits unless the filter is JIT-compiled, so `=` misses matching rows and `>`/`<`
return extra rows. BETWEEN loses the digits even with the JIT. Literals in every other year keep them.

### To reproduce

```sql
CREATE TABLE x (k SYMBOL, v TIMESTAMP_NS, ts TIMESTAMP) TIMESTAMP(ts) PARTITION BY DAY;
INSERT INTO x VALUES
  ('max', '2262-04-11T23:47:16.854775807Z', '1970-01-01T00:00:00.000000Z'),
  ('mid', '2262-01-01T00:00:00.000000123Z', '1970-01-01T00:00:01.000000Z');

-- with cairo.sql.jit.mode=off, on an ARM64 server, or with parallel filtering off:
SELECT k FROM x WHERE v = '2262-04-11T23:47:16.854775807Z';
SELECT k FROM x WHERE v > '2262-01-01T00:00:00.000000200Z';
-- in every mode:
SELECT k FROM x WHERE v BETWEEN '2262-04-11T23:47:16.854775807Z' AND '2262-04-11T23:47:16.854775807Z';
```

Actual: the first query returns no rows, the second returns `max` and `mid`, the third returns no rows.
`EXPLAIN` shows the bound as `2262-04-11T23:47:16.854775Z`.
Expected: `max` for each query, the same result with the JIT on and off.

Likely cause: `FunctionParser.getAdaptiveTimestampType()` parses a timestamp literal at nanosecond precision only
when its year is below 2262, so every 2262 literal is parsed as microseconds and truncated, although
TIMESTAMP_NS reaches 2262-04-11T23:47:16.854775807Z. The JIT parses the literal itself with the column's
precision, which is why it gets the right answer for `=`, `<` and `>`.

Reproduced on master e4da5b4543.

<details>
<summary>Failing test</summary>

Add to `core/src/test/java/io/questdb/test/griffin/CompiledFilterTest.java`:

```java
    @Test
    public void testTimestampNsTextBoundInYear2262() throws Exception {
        // TIMESTAMP_NS ends at 2262-04-11T23:47:16.854775807Z. A text bound in 2262 with
        // sub-microsecond digits must keep them in the Java filter as well as in the JIT.
        assertMemoryLeak(() -> {
            execute("CREATE TABLE x (k SYMBOL, v TIMESTAMP_NS, ts TIMESTAMP) TIMESTAMP(ts) PARTITION BY DAY");
            execute("""
                    INSERT INTO x VALUES
                    ('max', '2262-04-11T23:47:16.854775807Z', '1970-01-01T00:00:00.000000Z'),
                    ('mid', '2262-01-01T00:00:00.000000123Z', '1970-01-01T00:00:01.000000Z'),
                    ('one', '1970-01-01T00:00:00.000000001Z', '1970-01-01T00:00:02.000000Z')
                    """);

            final String eqMax = "SELECT k FROM x WHERE v = '2262-04-11T23:47:16.854775807Z'";
            for (int mode = 0; mode < 3; mode++) {
                final boolean isParallel = mode > 0;
                final boolean isJit = mode == 2;
                sqlExecutionContext.setParallelFilterEnabled(isParallel);
                sqlExecutionContext.setJitMode(isJit ? SqlJitMode.JIT_MODE_ENABLED : SqlJitMode.JIT_MODE_DISABLED);
                try (RecordCursorFactory factory = select(eqMax)) {
                    Assert.assertEquals(isJit, factory.usesCompiledFilter());
                }

                assertQuery(eqMax).noLeakCheck().returns("k\nmax\n");
                assertQuery("SELECT k FROM x WHERE v = '2262-01-01T00:00:00.000000123Z'")
                        .noLeakCheck().returns("k\nmid\n");
                assertQuery("SELECT k FROM x WHERE v > '2262-01-01T00:00:00.000000200Z'")
                        .noLeakCheck().returns("k\nmax\n");
                assertQuery("SELECT k FROM x WHERE v <= '2262-01-01T00:00:00.000000122Z'")
                        .noLeakCheck().returns("k\none\n");
