body_text:
A CASE expression (or `coalesce()`) that mixes a CHAR value, a SYMBOL value and an IPv4 or UUID value
is rejected when the CHAR value comes first, but is accepted and then fails while reading rows when the
SYMBOL value comes first. The result of a CASE should not depend on the order of its branches.

### To reproduce

```sql
CREATE TABLE t (b BOOLEAN, c CHAR, s SYMBOL, ip IPv4);
INSERT INTO t VALUES (true, 'x', 'sym', '1.2.3.4'), (false, 'y', 'abc', '1.2.3.4');

SELECT CASE WHEN b THEN c WHEN NOT b THEN s ELSE ip END r FROM t;
SELECT CASE WHEN b THEN s WHEN NOT b THEN c ELSE ip END r FROM t;
```

Actual: the first query fails with `inconvertible types: IPv4 -> SYMBOL`; the second is accepted and
fails while reading rows with `UnsupportedOperationException`. `coalesce(c, s, ip)` and
`coalesce(s, c, ip)` behave the same way, and so do UUID values in place of IPv4.
Expected: both orders behave the same.

Likely cause: `CaseCommon` picks the result type by folding the branch types left to right through a
fixed table, and that table maps CHAR then SYMBOL to SYMBOL but SYMBOL then CHAR to STRING. Every other
pair of text types in the table gives the same answer in both orders (for example SYMBOL with STRING
gives STRING). A second problem shows up in the order that is accepted: when no conversion exists from a
branch's type to the result type (here SYMBOL or CHAR to IPv4), `CaseCommon.getCastFunction()` keeps the
branch unconverted, so reading it as IPv4 fails at run time. The same happens with
`CASE WHEN b THEN s WHEN NOT b THEN '5.6.7.8' ELSE ip END` in any order.

Reproduced on master e4da5b4543.

<details>
<summary>Failing test</summary>

Add to `core/src/test/java/io/questdb/test/griffin/engine/functions/conditional/CaseCommonTest.java`:

```java
private static final int NO_COMMON_TYPE = -1;

@Test
public void testCommonTypeIsSymmetric() {
    // CASE, SWITCH and coalesce fold their branch types left to right, so the common
    // type of a pair must not depend on which branch comes first.
    for (short l = MIN_COLUMN_TYPE_ID; l <= MAX_COLUMN_TYPE_ID; l++) {
        for (short r = MIN_COLUMN_TYPE_ID; r <= MAX_COLUMN_TYPE_ID; r++) {
            Assert.assertEquals(
                    "l = " + ColumnType.nameOf(l) + ", r = " + ColumnType.nameOf(r),
                    getCommonTypeOrNone(l, r),
                    getCommonTypeOrNone(r, l)
            );
        }
    }
    Assert.assertEquals(ColumnType.STRING, getCommonTypeOrNone(ColumnType.CHAR, ColumnType.SYMBOL));
}

private static int getCommonTypeOrNone(short l, short r) {
    try {
        return CaseCommon.getCommonType(l, r, 0, "UNDEFINED is not expected");
    } catch (SqlException e) {
        return NO_COMMON_TYPE;
    }
}
```

On master it fails with: `java.lang.AssertionError: l = CHAR, r = SYMBOL expected:<12> but was:<11>`

</details>

