## Summary

`areHookInputsEqual` in `packages/react-reconciler/src/ReactFiberHooks.js` compares dependencies only up to `min(prevDeps.length, nextDeps.length)`:

```js
for (let i = 0; i < prevDeps.length && i < nextDeps.length; i++) {
  if (is(nextDeps[i], prevDeps[i])) continue;
  return false;
}
return true;
```

When the deps array grows between renders, the extra entries are never compared, so the hook treats the inputs as unchanged and keeps the previous value.

## Reproduction

React 19.3.0, `react-test-renderer`, Node (development build):

```js
const React = require('react');
const TestRenderer = require('react-test-renderer');
const {useMemo} = React;

let computeCount = 0;
let r;
function App({a, b}) {
  const value = useMemo(
    () => { computeCount++; return `a=${a} b=${b}`; },
    a === undefined ? [] : b === undefined ? [a] : [a, b],
  );
  r = value;
  return null;
}

let root;
TestRenderer.act(() => { root = TestRenderer.create(React.createElement(App, {a: 1})); });
TestRenderer.act(() => root.update(React.createElement(App, {a: 1, b: 2})));
console.log(JSON.stringify(r), computeCount);
```

- **Actual:** `"a=1 b=undefined" 1`
- **Expected:** `"a=1 b=2" 2`

## Notes

- In development, React already logs "changed size between renders", so this only happens when user code breaks the rules of hooks.
- Possible fix: return `false` when the two deps arrays have different lengths, in both development and production.

