React version: 19.3.0-canary-b618bbb4-20261007 (main as of 2026-10-07). Earlier canaries behave as expected, see the bisect below.

Since #37739, a synchronous error-recovery retry that is only interrupted for selective hydration turns error recovery off. The error boundary then commits for an error that the retry would have recovered from.

## Steps To Reproduce

1. `npm i react@canary react-dom@canary jsdom`
2. Save the script below as `repro.cjs` and run `node repro.cjs`. `NODE_ENV=production` gives the same result.

An effect schedules an urgent update (`setIndex(2)`) and a transition (`setItems([...])`) that are only consistent together. The concurrent render of the urgent update throws. This is the kind of data race the synchronous retry exists for. The same transition also updates state inside a Suspense boundary that has not hydrated yet (`setTheme('dark')`).

Link to code example:

```js
// npm i react@canary react-dom@canary jsdom && node repro.cjs
const { JSDOM } = require('jsdom');
const dom = new JSDOM('<!doctype html><html><body></body></html>');
for (const key of ['window', 'document', 'navigator', 'Node', 'HTMLElement', 'Element', 'Event', 'HTMLIFrameElement']) {
  globalThis[key] = key === 'window' ? dom.window : dom.window[key];
}
const React = require('react');
const { hydrateRoot } = require('react-dom/client');
const { renderToReadableStream } = require('react-dom/server.browser');
const h = React.createElement;

class ErrorBoundary extends React.Component {
  state = { error: null };
  static getDerivedStateFromError(error) { return { error }; }
  render() { return this.state.error ? `Something went wrong: ${this.state.error.message}` : this.props.children; }
}

let setTheme;
function Row({ items, index }) {
  // Torn read: the index arrives in an urgent update, the list in a transition.
  if (index >= items.length) throw new Error(`index ${index} of ${items.length}`);
  return items[index];
}
function List() {
  const [items, setItems] = React.useState(['a']);
  const [index, setIndex] = React.useState(0);
  React.useEffect(() => {
    React.startTransition(() => {
      setItems(['a', 'b', 'c']);
      setTheme('dark'); // remove this line and the error is recovered
    });
    setIndex(2);
  }, []);
  return h(ErrorBoundary, null, h(Row, { items, index }));
}
function Theme({ theme }) { return h('span', null, `theme:${theme}`); }
function Sidebar() {
  const [theme, set] = React.useState('light');
  setTheme = set;
  // Still dehydrated when the transition above updates it.
  return h(React.Suspense, { fallback: 'Loading...' }, h(Theme, { theme }));
}
const App = () => h(React.Fragment, null, h(List), '|', h(Sidebar));

(async () => {
  const stream = await renderToReadableStream(h(App));
  await stream.allReady;
  const container = document.createElement('div');
  container.innerHTML = await new Response(stream).text();
  document.body.appendChild(container);
  const log = [];
  hydrateRoot(container, h(App), {
    onCaughtError: (e) => log.push(`onCaughtError: ${e.message}`),
    onUncaughtError: (e) => log.push(`onUncaughtError: ${e.message}`),
    onRecoverableError: (e) => log.push(`onRecoverableError: ${e.message.split('.')[0]}`),
  });
  await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 500));
  console.log(`react-dom ${require('react-dom/package.json').version}`);
  console.log(`text: ${container.textContent}`);
  for (const line of log) console.log(line);
  process.exit(0); // the scheduler's MessageChannel keeps Node alive
})();
```

## The current behavior

```
react-dom 19.3.0-canary-b618bbb4-20261007
text: Something went wrong: index 2 of 1|theme:dark
onCaughtError: index 2 of 1
```

Without an error boundary, the root unmounts (`onUncaughtError`).

## The expected behavior

```
text: c|theme:dark
onRecoverableError: There was an error during concurrent rendering but React was able to recover by instead synchronously rendering the entire root
```

Bisect, same script, development and production builds alike:

| build | result |
| --- | --- |
| 19.3.0-canary-92f4fda3-20260629 (before #36911) | recovers |
| 19.3.0-canary-7c6ac13e-20260929 (#36911) | recovers |
| 19.3.0-canary-b618bbb4-20261007 (#36911 + #37739) | error boundary |

Control: without `setTheme('dark')` in the transition, b618bbb4 recovers too (`c|theme:light` plus `onRecoverableError`).

## What I think happens

`recoverFromConcurrentError` retries synchronously with the pending lanes, transition included. That retry would render both updates and succeed. During the retry, the transition's update reaches the dehydrated boundary, so `renderRootSync` stops with `SuspendedOnHydration`. It resets the stack and returns `RootSuspendedAtTheShell` from a local variable ([ReactFiberWorkLoop.js#L2688-L2698](https://github.com/react/react/blob/b618bbb4422693bbb8d387180687b3f510300f42/packages/react-reconciler/src/ReactFiberWorkLoop.js#L2688-L2698)).

Since #37739, `recoverFromConcurrentError` reads that status as "the retry suspended in the shell" and adds the errored lanes to `errorRecoveryDisabledLanes` ([#L1354-L1373](https://github.com/react/react/blob/b618bbb4422693bbb8d387180687b3f510300f42/packages/react-reconciler/src/ReactFiberWorkLoop.js#L1354-L1373)). But nothing suspended in the shell: `workInProgressRootExitStatus` is not `RootSuspendedAtTheShell`, and `root.shellSuspendCounter` did not move ([#L2748](https://github.com/react/react/blob/b618bbb4422693bbb8d387180687b3f510300f42/packages/react-reconciler/src/ReactFiberWorkLoop.js#L2748)). Once the boundary has hydrated, the next render of those lanes commits the error boundary.

## A possible fix

Keep recovery on when the retry was only interrupted for selective hydration:

```js
const shellSuspendsBeforeRetry = root.shellSuspendCounter;
const exitStatus = renderRootSync(root, errorRetryLanes, false);
if (exitStatus === RootSuspendedAtTheShell) {
  const onlyInterruptedForHydration =
    workInProgressRootExitStatus !== RootSuspendedAtTheShell &&
    root.shellSuspendCounter === shellSuspendsBeforeRetry;
  if (!wasRootDehydrated && !onlyInterruptedForHydration) {
    root.errorRecoveryDisabledLanes = mergeLanes(
      root.errorRecoveryDisabledLanes,
      mergeLanes(originallyAttemptedLanes, workInProgressDeferredLane),
    );
  }
}
```

Each check covers a case the other misses. I tried each one alone on b618bbb4:

- **Counter.** A retry can suspend in the shell (the global status stays `RootSuspendedWithDelay` while it prerenders siblings) and then be stopped by selective hydration. The counter moved, so recovery is disabled and the boundary commits at once, as on b618bbb4. With the status check alone, the error stays hidden until the next update.
- **Status.** A thrown object whose `then` getter returns `undefined` on the read in `handleThrow` and a function on the read in `throwException` suspends the shell without counting. The global status is `RootSuspendedAtTheShell`, so recovery is disabled, as on b618bbb4. With the counter check alone, the error never showed, even after the next update. Ordinary promises don't do this; it takes a stateful getter.

I applied this to the built `react-dom-client.{development,production}.js` of b618bbb4. Apart from the repro script, these ran under a mock scheduler in three modes: all tasks flushed at once, one task per macrotask, and host-paced.

- The repro above: recovers in both builds, with and without the error boundary.
- #37739's own case: `useDeferredValue` whose initial value suspends in the shell and whose final value throws. The patched build settles on the error boundary after one retry, as b618bbb4 does. With #36911 alone it loops.
- Six other trees with the same race. They cover a click during partial hydration without an error boundary, a boundary that is still streaming, a boundary hydrated later at offscreen priority without an error boundary, and two router-shaped trees: a refresh under a loading boundary, and a server action while the page streams. All six recover as with #36911 alone. On b618bbb4, each one shows the boundary or unmounts the root.

I have not run React's own test suite against this change.

There is a cost for real errors that no retry can fix. When the first retry is only interrupted for hydration, the patched build runs a second retry where b618bbb4 gives up, and in two cases that changes what the user sees:

- If the second retry suspends in the shell, recovery is disabled there. The error boundary then appears on the next update or ping, not right after hydration, which is how #36911 alone behaved.
- If a transition in the second retry suspends inside a visible Suspense boundary, the error boundary commits together with that boundary's fallback. b618bbb4 keeps the old content until the data arrives. A client root without hydration shows the fallback the same way on all three builds.

I found this while backporting #36911 and #37739 to the React DOM that Next.js 15.5 vendors.

